    subcategorySlug: "design-fundamentals",
    experienceLevel: "FRESHER",
    difficulty: "EASY",
    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "Raster graphics are made of a fixed grid of pixels, so they're resolution-dependent - enlarging a raster image beyond its native resolution makes it blurry or pixelated. Vector graphics are based on mathematical paths and can be scaled without losing quality.",
    explanation: "",
    sampleAnswer: "Raster graphics are made of a fixed grid of pixels, so they're resolution-dependent - enlarging a raster image beyond its native resolution makes it blurry or pixelated. Vector graphics are defined by mathematical paths, curves, and fills rather than pixels, so they can be scaled to any size without losing sharpness. Raster is the natural format for photographs and detailed, continuous-tone imagery; vector is preferred for logos, icons, and illustrations that need to work cleanly at many different sizes, from a favicon to a billboard.",
    detailedAnswer: "The difference between raster and vector graphics is mainly how they represent and store visual information.\n\n### Raster graphics\n\nRaster graphics are made up of a grid of individual pixels. Each pixel stores color information, and the image has a fixed resolution such as 1920 × 1080 pixels.\n\nCommon raster formats include JPEG, PNG, WebP, and GIF.\n\nRaster graphics are best for images that contain photographic detail, subtle gradients, textures, and complex color variations. Examples include photographs, screenshots, digital paintings, and realistic images.\n\nThe main limitation of raster graphics is that they are resolution-dependent. If you enlarge a raster image beyond its original resolution, the software has to interpolate or create additional pixels. As a result, edges can become blurry and individual pixels may become visible.\n\n### Vector graphics\n\nVector graphics are created using mathematical paths, curves, lines, shapes, fills, and strokes rather than a fixed grid of pixels.\n\nCommon vector formats include SVG, AI, and EPS.\n\nBecause the artwork is mathematically defined, it can be scaled up or down without losing its sharpness. This makes vector graphics particularly useful for logos, icons, diagrams, illustrations, and other designs that need to appear at many different sizes.\n\nFor example, a company logo might need to appear as a small website icon, on a business card, and on a large billboard. A vector master file can be used to produce all of those versions while keeping the edges clean and sharp.\n\n### Quick comparison\n\nRaster graphics are pixel-based, resolution-dependent, and generally best for photographs and detailed imagery. Vector graphics are path-based, resolution-independent, and generally best for logos, icons, diagrams, and scalable illustrations.\n\n### A simple way to remember it\n\nThink of a raster image like a mosaic made from tiny colored tiles. If you make the mosaic much larger, the individual tiles eventually become visible.\n\nA vector image is more like a set of drawing instructions. Instead of storing every pixel, it tells the computer where to draw lines, curves, and shapes. The computer can redraw those elements at different sizes while maintaining clean edges.\n\n### When would you use each?\n\nUse raster graphics when you are working with photographs, realistic images, complex textures, detailed digital artwork, or images that require pixel-level editing.\n\nUse vector graphics when you are designing logos, icons, symbols, diagrams, illustrations, or artwork that needs to be resized across different applications while maintaining sharp edges.\n\n### Practical interview example\n\nSuppose a startup asks a designer to create its new logo. The designer would normally create the master artwork as a vector graphic. From that single source, the designer could export a small favicon, a website logo, a social-media graphic, a business-card version, and a large trade-show banner.\n\nIf the original logo existed only as a small raster PNG, enlarging it for the banner could make the edges blurry or pixelated. The designer would need a higher-resolution source or would have to recreate the artwork as vector artwork.\n\n### Important nuance\n\nRaster and vector are not simply good and bad formats. The right choice depends on the type of artwork and how it will be used.\n\nFor example, a photograph placed inside a vector-based design document is still fundamentally raster image content. Similarly, formats such as PDF can contain both vector and raster elements, so the file extension alone does not always tell you whether the content is raster or vector.\n\n### Interview takeaway\n\nA strong interview answer is: Raster graphics are pixel-based and are best for photographs and detailed imagery, but they are resolution-dependent and can become pixelated when enlarged. Vector graphics use mathematical paths and shapes, so they can scale without losing quality and are therefore ideal for logos, icons, illustrations, diagrams, and other scalable artwork.",
